    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 21 with a thin slate, as several core assets like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Trea Turner** are sidelined by team off-days. Consequently, the focus shifts to our top projected starter, **Kazuma Okamoto** (65.31), who draws a favorable matchup against Carlos Rod\u00f3n. **Kazuma Okamoto** benefits significantly from the platoon advantage against the southpaw, with his score bolstered by an aggressive +7% platoon boost and an assumed clean-up spot in the order. Similarly, **Dillon Dingler** (60.32) remains a high-value play; his ability to capitalize on the dynamic platoon advantage against Joey Cantillo, combined with his elevated spot in the order at #3, makes him our most efficient bat of the day despite a moderate xERA of 4.03 for the opposing starter.\n\n**Carson Benge** (57.23) teeters on the edge of our \"Strong\" start threshold. While his raw metrics are solid, he faces a challenge against Jake Cavalli, whose 3.99 xERA suggests he is the toughest arm on today\u2019s abbreviated board. We are riding **Carson Benge** primarily due to his leadoff role and the +7% platoon edge, but he carries the highest volatility risk among our active starters. Should our other depth pieces like **Kyle Schwarber**, **Christian Yelich**, or **Caleb Durbin** have been available, the rotation would have looked significantly different; however, with roster flexibility constrained by the off-day, we are essentially forced into this specific configuration. Keep a close eye on the official lineup card for **Kazuma Okamoto**, as any drop out of the #4 hole would necessitate a recalibration of his expected run-producing potential. No other bench candidates from the remaining available pool\u2014such as **Hyeseong Kim**, **Colt Keith**, or **Zac Veen**\u2014were able to bridge the efficiency gap to displace our current starters.",
